<h1>About this demo — Blazor Auto</h1>

<h2>The three samples</h2>
<p>
    Every playground in this repository is written once, in <code>Demos.Shared</code>, and hosted by
    three sibling apps — one per Blazor hosting model. Running the same components three ways is the
    point: it is how a component that quietly depends on one model gets caught.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppSvrDemo</strong> — Blazor Server, everything over a SignalR circuit.</li>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppAutoDemo</strong> — Blazor Auto. <em>You are here.</em></li>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppWasmDemo</strong> — standalone WebAssembly, no server host at all.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What this one demonstrates</h2>
<p>
    Every page here is marked <code>@@rendermode InteractiveAuto</code>. On a first visit the
    component runs interactively <em>on the server</em> while the WebAssembly runtime downloads in
    the background; once it is cached, later visits run the same component <em>in the browser</em>.
    That makes this the only sample where one component is exercised by both runtimes — and the one
    place a difference between them can surface.
</p>
<p>
    It genuinely does surface. <code>AtomQrCodeImage</code> branches on
    <code>OperatingSystem.IsBrowser()</code>: the server pass decodes with managed ImageSharp, the
    WebAssembly pass hands the work to a native <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> read, because ImageSharp
    is punishingly slow in the interpreted WebAssembly runtime. Load
    <a href="QRImage">the QR image viewer</a> cold, then reload it, to see both paths.
</p>
<p>
    Shape: a host project plus a <code>.Client</code> project. Pages live in <code>.Client</code>
    precisely so both runtimes can load the same assembly, and the host registers both interactive
    render modes.
</p>

